window.docContents[151].resolve({"/Tactics/#Logical-Foundations--Tactics___-More-Basic-Tactics--Using--cases--on-Expressions":{"contents":"Is this worth adding somewhere around here, given that we have\njust introduced injectivity and disjointness tactics, and that we are\nnow talking about the generalized use of cases ?Logically, induction subsumes case analysis —\nyou can simply ignore those inductive hypotheses\nso anything provable by case analysis is also provable\nusing the induction principle.However, Lean's cases has specialized machinery for indexed inductive families.\nHere are some examples that cases can solve while induction can't:-- substitution\nexample (x : Nat) (h : x = 0) : Nat.succ x = 1 := by\n  -- induction h\n  cases h\n  rfl\n-- disjointness\nexample (h : (0 : Nat) = 1) : False := by\n  -- induction h\n  cases h\n-- injectivity\nexample {m n : Nat} (h : Nat.succ m = Nat.succ n) : m = n := by\n  -- induction h\n  cases h\n  rfl\n-- acyclicity\nexample (n : Nat) (h : n = Nat.succ n) : False := by\n  -- induction h\n  cases h\n... and there are more!\n\n\n\nThe cases tactic can be used on expressions as well as\nvariables:\n\ndef chooseIf {α : Type} (test : α → Bool) (x y : α) : α :=\n  if test x then x else y\n\ntheorem chooseIf_self {α : Type} (test : α → Bool) (x : α) :\n    chooseIf test x x = x := by\n  rw [chooseIf]\n  cases test x <;> rfl\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009Tactics: More Basic Tactics","header":"7.7. Using  cases  on Expressions","id":"/Tactics/#Logical-Foundations--Tactics___-More-Basic-Tactics--Using--cases--on-Expressions"}});